# Task breakdown

Turns a requirements deliverable into a board of cards a build session can pick
up cold. It is the bridge between requirements gathering and building.

## What it produces

- One card per unit of work, each independently executable.
- A backlog index document listing the cards, their waves and their dependencies.

## Cardinal rules

1. **One session, one agent, one card.** If a card cannot plausibly be finished
   in one session using less than half a context window, it is too big — split it.
2. **Every card names its source.** A card with no link back to the requirements
   document is unreviewable.
3. **Every card names its files.** Guessing which files to touch is the single
   largest source of wasted sessions.
4. **Every card states what it must NOT do.** Scope creep is prevented at the
   card, not in the session.
5. **No card invents requirements.** If the requirements are silent, the card
   says so and the question goes back to the owner.

## Phase 0 — Load the deliverable

Read the requirements document end to end before splitting anything. Extract the
numbered chunks it already proposes, and note the acceptance criteria attached
to each. Verify any path, flag or command name it cites against the live tree —
requirements documents go stale, and the source wins.

## Phase 1 — Size every unit

Sort each chunk S, M, L or XL by the work one agent can verify in one session.

- **Split an XL** along the seam that produces two independently testable
  halves — usually a data-layer change and the surface that consumes it. Never
  split so that neither half can be verified alone.
- **Merge trivial units** only when they touch the same files; two small cards
  in different files stay two cards.
- **Order into waves.** Wave 1 is everything with no dependency. A card in wave
  N names the cards it depends on, and depends only on earlier waves.

## Phase 2 — Write the cards

Each card carries, in this order:

- **Objective** — one sentence, in the imperative.
- **Source** — the requirements document and the section it comes from.
- **Target** — the repository and the surface being changed.
- **Files to touch** — concrete paths; mark any that is a guess.
- **Acceptance criteria** — checkable statements, each with the command that
  checks it.
- **Out of scope** — the adjacent work this card must not do.
- **Depends on** — card ids, or none.
- **Single-session guarantee** — one line saying why this fits in one session.
- **How to run it** — the exact commands to build and verify.

Create the cards on the board when a board is reachable; otherwise write them
into the backlog document and hand the owner the list.

## Phase 3 — Backlog index

One document listing every card by wave, with its one-line objective and its
dependencies, and a short note on what the breakdown deliberately deferred.
Give it `answers:` frontmatter so a future session can find it.

## Anti-patterns

- A card whose acceptance criterion is "it works".
- A card that depends on a later wave.
- A card that spans repositories with no seam between them.
- A backlog that renumbers on every run — card ids are stable once issued.
